      "dom_id": "question-51883",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251848/assets/fa895816cc537fc2ccb30f99f2f9c8ec01cf0ee75ba76fc8a2aed1c0b3349206.png)\n\nThe graph above shows that the volume of gas $L$ in a truck's tank is directly proportional to the time $t$ since the driver started filling it. Find a direct variation equation that describes the relationship between $L$ and $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $L$ varies directly with $t,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nL=kt.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that there is a point on the graph with $t=6$ and $L=90.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =k\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{90}{6} & =k \\\\ 15 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nL=15t.\n$$",
