      "dom_id": "question-68193",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251848/assets/2501f7e346abf6c79a211fd090162be685c001bc6acc68787d8c73c0eee7a8af.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship between the weight $x$ (in pounds) of ice cream sold by a store and the profit $y$ (in dollars).  What is the constant of proportionality?",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(3,4).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251848/assets/7beacc5836ed16a4dcb36916d0273513b7b2d94871c4ee5994d56479bc11f18d.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}=1\\,\\text{R}1=1\\,\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ dollars per pound.",
